  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products in a wide range of categories [10-K line 00134]. It operates with a no-frills, self-service warehouse format designed for efficiency in selling space, merchandise handling, and inventory control [10-K line 00139]. Costco buys most merchandise directly from suppliers, routing it through cross-docking consolidation points or directly to warehouses [10-K line 00135]. The company operates with a membership model where members pay annual fees ($65 in the U.S.) and can utilize memberships at all warehouses and e-commerce sites [10-K line 00157].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with global, national, regional wholesalers and retailers including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and operators selling narrow ranges of merchandise [10-K line 00190]. Significant competitors in the U.S. include Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon, as well as other warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190]. Costco's competitive advantage includes its low prices, quality merchandise selection, membership model, and operational efficiency [10-K line 00134].\n\n## Growth Drivers\nThe primary growth driver for Costco is increasing net sales, particularly comparable sales [MD&A line 00398]. Comparable sales are achieved through increased shopping frequency from new and existing members and the amount they spend per visit (average ticket) [MD&A line 00398]. The company also grows through opening new warehouses, with a focus on international markets where there is more room for expansion [MD&A line 00405]. Additionally, Costco benefits from its membership fee revenue, which increased by 11% in 2026, driven by new member sign-ups, fee increases, and upgrades to Executive memberships [MD&A line 00459].\n\n## Catalysts\nKey catalysts for growth include the expansion of e-commerce operations, which represented 11% of total net sales in 2026 [10-K line 00153], and digitally-enabled sales, which increased by 21% in 2026 [MD&A line 00452]. Rising gasoline prices positively impacted net sales by $3.5 billion in 2026, contributing to a 130 basis point increase in net sales [MD&A line 00454]. The company's ability to maintain high member renewal rates (92.3% in U.S. and Canada) also serves as a catalyst [MD&A line 00459].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn 2026, Costco opened 28 new warehouses, including three relocations, for a total of 25 net new warehouses [MD&A line 00415]. Net sales increased by 10% to $297.2 billion in 2026, driven by an increase in comparable sales and sales at new warehouses [MD&A line 00416]. The company reported a 14% increase in net income to $9.226 billion, with earnings per diluted share reaching $20.76 [MD&A line 00422]. In April 2026, the Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [MD&A line 00423].\n\n## Material Risks\nCostco faces risks related to economic conditions including inflation or deflation, which can impact consumer spending and cost of goods [MD&A line 00398]. Additionally, tariffs imposed by governments affect the costs of merchandise, with higher tariffs potentially adversely impacting results [MD&A line 00403]. The company also faces risks related to employee compensation and benefits, which are a significant portion of operating expenses [10-K line 00172]. There is also risk from currency fluctuations that can impact international operations [MD&A line 00409]. Finally, while Costco has a strong member retention rate, membership growth in certain international markets and online sales may renew at lower rates on average [MD&A line 00406].",
